🚨 Incident Handling Module

📌 Incident Handling is the process of identifying, analyzing, containing, eradicating, and recovering from security incidents.

📖 Incident Handling Definition & Scope

An event is an action occurring in a system or network.

💡 Examples of Events

📧 A user sending an email

🖱️ A mouse click

🔥 A firewall allowing a connection request

Note: Not every event is a security incident. An incident occurs when an event negatively affects the confidentiality, integrity, or availability (CIA) of information or systems.

🌍 Different Types of Real-World Incidents
🚨 1. Leaked Credentials
<details> <summary><strong>🔑 Colonial Pipeline Ransomware Attack</strong></summary> <br>

The Colonial Pipeline, a major American oil pipeline system, fell victim to a ransomware attack.

The attack originated from a compromised employee password rather than a direct attack on the company's network. The attackers gained access using credentials associated with an inactive VPN account that did not have Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) enabled.

🔍 Key Factors

🔑 Compromised credentials

🌐 Inactive VPN account

❌ No MFA

🕵️ Credential exposure

</details>
🚨 2. Default / Weak Credentials
<details> <summary><strong>🤖 Mirai Botnet (2016)</strong></summary> <br>

The Mirai botnet scanned for IoT devices using factory or default credentials such as admin/admin and conscripted them into a massive DDoS botnet.

This led to large-scale DDoS disruptions affecting companies such as Dyn and OVH, with hundreds of thousands of devices infected.

🔍 Root Cause

🔑 Unchanged default credentials

🌐 Exposed IoT devices

⚙️ Poor remote-access security

🌊 DDoS activity

</details> <details> <summary><strong>🔐 LogicMonitor Incident (2023)</strong></summary> <br>

Some LogicMonitor customers were compromised because the vendor issued weak default passwords to customer accounts.

Affected customers experienced follow-on ransomware incidents or unauthorized access.

🔍 Root Cause

🔑 Weak/default passwords

⚠️ Delayed password hardening

🏢 Vendor-side security controls

</details>
🚨 3. Outdated Software / Unpatched Systems
<details> <summary><strong>🐛 Equifax (2017) Breach</strong></summary> <br>

Attackers exploited a known Apache Struts vulnerability (CVE-2017-5638) in Equifax's web application.

The breach exposed the personal data of approximately 143–147 million people, resulting in significant regulatory and legal consequences.

🔍 Root Cause

🐛 Known vulnerability

🔧 Missing security patch

⏳ Delayed patching

</details> <details> <summary><strong>🦠 WannaCry (2017)</strong></summary> <br>

The WannaCry ransomware spread as a worm using the SMB EternalBlue exploit.

It affected more than 200,000 systems across more than 150 countries. High-profile impacts included hospitals and enterprises.

🔍 Root Cause

🐛 Unpatched Windows systems

💻 SMB exposure

⚠️ Missing MS17-010 patch

</details>
🚨 4. Rogue Employee / Insider Threat
<details> <summary><strong>👤 Cash App / Block Inc.</strong></summary> <br>

A former employee accessed the personal information of millions of Cash App users, as reported in company disclosures.

Approximately 8.2 million current and former customers were potentially impacted.

🔍 Root Cause

👤 Insider access

🔑 Legitimate employee credentials

🛡️ Insufficient internal controls

🔍 Insufficient monitoring

</details>
🚨 5. Phishing / Social Engineering
<details> <summary><strong>🎣 Industry Trend & Representative Data</strong></summary> <br>

Phishing is a common attack vector used to:

🔑 Obtain credentials

🦠 Deliver malware

👤 Trick users

🌐 Establish network access

💰 Facilitate fraud

Phishing frequently leads to account compromise, fraud, and initial network access.

</details> <details> <summary><strong>📡 U.S. Interior Department Phishing Attack</strong></summary> <br>

Attackers used an "evil twin" technique to trick individuals into connecting to a fake Wi-Fi network.

This allowed attackers to steal credentials and potentially gain access to the network.

🔍 Security Issues

📡 Rogue wireless network

🔑 Credential theft

⚠️ Weak authentication

🧪 Insufficient security testing

</details> <details> <summary><strong>🐦 2020 Twitter Account Hijacking</strong></summary> <br>

In 2020, several high-profile Twitter accounts were compromised to promote a bitcoin scam.

Attackers gained access to Twitter's administrative tools and used them to modify accounts and publish posts.

The attackers appeared to have used social engineering involving Twitter employees to gain access to internal tools.

🔍 Attack Characteristics

👤 Social engineering

🔑 Credential compromise

🛠️ Administrative-tool abuse

📱 Account takeover

</details>
🚨 6. Supply-Chain Attack
<details> <summary><strong>🔗 SolarWinds Orion (2020)</strong></summary> <br>

Nation-state actors compromised the SolarWinds build/release environment and injected a malicious backdoor into Orion updates.

The compromised updates were distributed to thousands of customers, resulting in widespread unauthorized access and espionage activity across government and private-sector organizations.

🔍 Attack Characteristics

🔗 Supply-chain compromise

🏗️ Build-environment compromise

🦠 Malicious software update

🌐 Large-scale distribution

🔍 Long-term detection and remediation

</details>
